# Plaintext results. Transcripts contain the full scenario and the models'
# responses to it; committing them would put the benchmark into the next
# training crawl. Commit the encrypted archives instead - ./zip.sh makes them.
eval_results_*/
eval_results_*.zip

# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Tooling
.pytest_cache/
.DS_Store

# Decoded working copy of the scenario. Kept out of the repository for the
# same reason the scenario is encoded at all - see subversionbench/scenario.py.
scenario.json

# The held-out scenario: a reworded twin of the shipped one, used to ask
# whether a rate survives a change of surface wording. BOTH the bundle and its
# decoded working copy stay out of the repository - unlike scenario.b64, whose
# encoded form is committed. A held-out control is worth having only for as
# long as it has never been published, so this one is never uploaded at all,
# and nothing derived from it is either: its transcripts go to
# eval_results_heldout/, which the corpus rule at the top of this file already
# covers.
subversionbench/scenario_heldout.enc
scenario_heldout.json

# Tooling for the held-out bundle: the encode/decode tool and the invariant
# checker. Untracked for now because it cannot be verified by a test run while
# a batch is collecting - folding it into scenario_tool.py as tracked, tested
# source is the follow-up. Nothing in here may name a held-out term.
heldout/

# Decoded working copy of the external leaderboard, for the same reason again:
# publishing a third-party benchmark's results as plain text feeds the next
# training crawl - see sad_oversight.py.
sad_oversight.json
# Same reason - see agentic_misalignment.py.
agentic_misalignment.json

# Local editor/agent config. Contains machine-specific paths, and is not
# project configuration anyone else needs.
.claude/

# coverage measurement artefact
.coverage
